course: dictionary
lesson: 2407
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: The word cut has many meanings, so you cannot just pick the first one you remember. Read the words around it to find the clues. «A message is cut into many small packets, each labelled with the address it must reach.» The words many small packets tell you that cut into means divided into pieces.
explain: Swap your meaning into the sentence to check it. «A combine harvester cuts the wheat, beats out the grains and blows away the straw at once.» Cuts means slices through the plants, so the wheat is cut down. Does that fit? Yes, it does.
explain: Money is another common meaning. «"Tax relief" means a tax cut.» «"Efficiency savings" means budget cuts (UK).» Here cut means a fall in an amount: less money is spent or charged. When you meet cut with a number or a price, ask yourself whether the amount is going down.
fact: Cut means to divide something, or take a piece off, with a knife or scissors.
fact: Cut can mean to make something shorter, as in «cut the grass», or to make less, as in «cut down on sugar».
fact: A cut is a small wound where the skin is opened, and it can also mean a fall in an amount, as in «a price cut».
fact: «Cut it out!» means stop it, and «didn't make the cut» means was not chosen.
fact: Exam writing often uses other words for cut: divide, sever, partition, subdivide and reduce.
exercise: «A message is cut into many small packets, each labelled with the address it must reach.» What does cut into mean here? | *Divided into pieces | Thrown away | Read aloud | Stuck together
exercise: «"Tax relief" means a tax cut.» What is a tax cut? | *A fall in the amount of tax | A tax form with a hole in it | A sharp knife for tax forms | A rise in the amount of tax
exercise: Vikings wrote with runes, letters of straight lines made for cutting into wood and stone. What is cutting doing to the wood and stone? | Washing them clean | Painting them bright colours | *Carving marks into the surface | Cooking the wood and stone
test: «Cut down on sugar» means | *Use less sugar | Eat more sugar | Buy a bigger sugar bowl | Bake a sugar cake
test: «Cut it out!» means | Come out of the house | *Stop it | Cut the paper now | Go faster
exam: In «a reduction of 20 per cent», which everyday word for cut could replace reduction? | *A cut | A slice of cake | A haircut at the salon | A short cut through the park
